A bug is in hand; this is where to look first. `superpowers:systematic-debugging` gives the phases and discipline; this skill gives the entry point.

## Before any hypothesis

1. **Reproducible → run it and read the output before naming a cause.** A hypothesis without a log is labelled a guess. A warning that also appears in a good run is not the cause. External-command wrappers: reproduce with the actual command, same shell, isolated environment.
2. **Hard to reproduce → narrow when → where → what, then fix.** For Windows UI bugs ask about monitor, zoom and DPI in the first questions.
3. **Never explain a reported defect away as intended.** A by-design answer rests on the artifact, not on a guess about the reason. Ask "what exactly did you launch" before theorising.
4. **Not reproducible for you → ask for an artifact** (screenshot, dump, exported file), not for remembered facts; questions only in terms of what the person sees. Refused → offer a joint reproduction: your instrument on before they act.
5. **Read what you already hold before spending the human's turn:** the dependency's sources, a connected reference corpus, your code, earlier dumps. Code you can run beats code you can read: run the counterparty's code on your exact input (its own test harness, your payload from outside its tree, its repo untouched) before writing it a request.

- A regression test guards the user's observed symptom; fixing a related technical defect does not close the complaint while the symptom remains.
- UI overlap: reproduce through the real entry path with earlier routes and panels open; target coordinates do not prove visibility.
- Flicker, disappearing element, lost focus: check the intermediate frames of the real action, not only after animations settle.
- Distinguish first load, background refresh over cached data, error and account switch; reproduce the transition that fails — a reset does not stand in for a refresh.

## Symptom → first move

| Symptom | First move | Not first |
|---|---|---|
| Blank where data should be | read the render and trace back: source, filters, defaults | template syntax, placeholders |
| A value arrives subtly wrong (space, case, date) | print the exact bytes sent, read the conversion | who calls whom |
| "X stays selected / dirty" | grep every write to that state | only the user's path |
| Partial state, no repro | read the order of steps — the survivor is an oracle | guessing where it aborted |
| Your own write reverts | grep write / regen / sync / refresh paths | repeating the write |
| Breaks while a sibling app on the same stack works | diff manifest, lockfile, config, binary assets | design or config variants |
| Fixed the reported path, symptom back elsewhere | grep the predicate by field name across sibling transports; fix all copies in one commit | closing on the named path |

## Operation-order oracle

For partial state you cannot reproduce, read the order in which the code would touch things. Survivor touched **first** → the code is exonerated, look for an external actor (human, agent, hook, process). Survivor touched **last** → an abort midway: an error, lock or early return (on Windows, a file lock during delete). A fix from this deduction alone is reported as deduced, not reproduced.

## At a boundary, check the representation first

A value crossing into a URL, JSON body, file name or header: ask what it turned into before tracing the flow. Numbers and dates are the tell — default stringification adds separators, locale dates, trailing zeros, and breaks only part of the range. Read a stringify at a boundary as a formatter. Tests send values past the formatter's threshold (four+ digits, a fraction).

## Your own edit reverts

From the second time, stop rewriting and find what overwrites it. A reminder that a file changed with no user action behind it is a signal. (Stale reads — IDE highlight, browser cache — are cured by a refresh.)

## The instrument lies

- Confirm environment assumptions by actual launch parameters and measurement; keep documented fact, local observation and hypothesis apart.
- An IDE warning after a refactor may be stale language-server state; the compiler, linter and grep decide.
- `cmd | tail` / `| head` / `| grep` returns the last stage's exit code — write to a file and check `$?` separately.
- A note "the tool can't do X" is dated; re-probe it before building a workaround.
- **A negative result needs the instrument to show a positive one first** — on an input that must say yes, and two inputs giving two answers. Uniform answers where conditions differ point at the instrument. A measured claim that changes a contract is re-derived with a second, different instrument before it is written.
- Before calling something a known limitation, list the unchecked options out loud (global rule "An unproven can't").

## A repaired mechanism does not prove coverage

A working function says nothing about the set it is called on. Before claiming it "will clear the backlog", open the selection that calls it and name what falls in and what does not.
